    "case_id": "730",
    "case_summary": "A 58-year-old male, with an unremarkable medical history, presented with a 2-year history of chest tightness on exertion and shortness of breath. He had two episodes of acute onset syncope with an unknown cause.\n\nNo specific physical examination findings were reported in the case.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. Multidetector computed tomography angiography:       Image Title: Ascending aortic aneurysm       Description: Revealed ascending aortic aneurysm with diameters of ascending aorta and sinus of Valsalva at 49mm and 40mm respectively        2. Transthoracic echocardiography:       Description: Revealed mild aortic regurgitation of the bicuspid aortic valve        3. Coronary angiography:       Description: Demonstrated slow blood perfusion in the left coronary artery, without evidence of significant atherosclerosis        4. Other tests:       - Transcranial Doppler (TCD): No abnormalities       - Ambulatory electrocardiography (Holter) monitoring: No abnormalities        5. Intraoperative findings:       Description: Organized thrombus measuring 1 × 0.8 cm found at the left coronary cusp of the aortic valve, covering most of the left coronary artery's ostium        6. Histopathological examination:       Description: Confirmed the excised mass as an organized thrombus",
